  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Improving conditions late today with noticeable \nwarmup and several days of dry conditions to end September and \nstart October \n\n850-700 mb thermal trough over the CWA this afternoon morning \nwill be exiting stage right tonight, allowing the cloud depth \nto shrink with gradual clearing early tonight as deep-layer\nsubsidence and slightly lower PWAT air advecting in from the \nwest.\n\nTemps +5-10 degrees warmer vs. Monday are near their max values\nthis mid afternoon hour...in the 65-75F range.\n\nLess cloud cover and light wind tonight will favor strong \nradiational cooling of the ground and locally dense valley fog \nwill form with very low visibility restrictions into early \nWednesday morning. \n\nA continue warmup will extend through the mid to late week\nperiod with fcst max temps in the 75-85F range or +10-15 \ndegrees above the historical normal for the end of \nSeptember/beginning of October. \n\nFor context, the average last date of max temp >=80F is 9/27 at\nState College, 9/29 at Altoona, 10/2 at Williamsport and 10/5 \nat Harrisburg. \n\nNo rainfall is forecast through Thursday with some showers\npossible on Friday-Friday night associated with cold front\nsinking southeastward from the eastern Great Lakes.  \n---------------------------------------------------------------\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2: Cooling trend this weekend into early next week \nwith an increasing chance of rain Saturday and Sunday. \n\n24hr maxT change on Saturday behind the cold front is fcst to \nbe 10-15 degrees lower vs. Friday with highs in the low 60s/70s.\nTrailing frontal wave could bring more widespread soaking rain\non Sunday based on WPC/NBM QPF - although EC/CMC and (to lesser\nextent) lean farther to the south/east with the frontal precip.\nConfidence is higher that temps continue to cool off and drop \nnear or below the historical average into early next week. Frost\npotential is on the table for next Monday and Tuesday night.",
